{"causes": ["The death of his father, Henry V the Fat, in 1296 led to Władysław's minority and an initial regency, setting the stage for future power struggles.", "The traditional Piast division of inheritance among male heirs created inherent instability and frequent fraternal disputes over ducal lands like Legnica, Brzeg, and Wrocław.", "The growing influence of the Teutonic Order in Central Europe provided an alternative path for noblemen, including dispossessed rulers, seeking new purpose or power."], "effects": ["Władysław's loss of Wrocław in 1311 further fragmented the Silesian Piast territories, strengthening his brother Henry VI the Good's control over the city.", "His renunciation of secular power and entry into the Teutonic Order in 1312 ended his ducal line and prevented the continuation of his branch of the Piast dynasty.", "The consolidation of Legnica under Bolesław III the Generous after 1312 shifted the balance of power within the Silesian duchies, impacting regional alliances."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "henry-v-the-fat", "sourceName": "Henry V the Fat", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "targetName": "Władysław of Legnica", "context": "The death of Henry V the Fat in 1296 directly influenced Władysław's early life as a minor duke under regency."}, {"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of Legnica", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "teutonic-order", "targetName": "Teutonic Order", "context": "Władysław of Legnica participated in the Teutonic Order by joining it as a knight in 1312 after losing his ducal lands."}, {"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of Legnica", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "silesia", "targetName": "Silesia", "context": "The ducal rule and life events of Władysław of Legnica primarily occurred in the historical region of Silesia during the late 13th and early 14th centuries."}, {"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of Legnica", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "fragmentation-of-silesian-piast-duchies", "targetName": "Fragmentation of Silesian Piast Duchies", "context": "Władysław's inability to retain his ducal lands and subsequent relinquishment contributed to the ongoing fragmentation of the Silesian Piast territories in the early 14th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "bolesław-iii-the-generous", "sourceName": "Bolesław III the Generous", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "targetName": "Władysław of Legnica", "context": "Bolesław III the Generous caused Władysław of Legnica to lose control of Legnica in 1312 through fraternal conflict and territorial redistribution."}], "places": [{"name": "Legnica, Poland", "role": "Primary Duchy"}, {"name": "Wrocław, Poland", "role": "Disputed Duchy"}, {"name": "Malbork, Poland", "role": "Teutonic Order Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Władysław of Legnica", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "władysław-of-legnica"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q558459", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_of_Legnica"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Władysław of Legnica's reign and subsequent entry into the Teutonic Order highlight the turbulent nature of Piast dynastic politics and the alternative paths available to dispossessed nobility in medieval Central Europe. His life serves as a specific case study of regional fragmentation and the influence of religious military orders.", "significanceCategory": "local"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-11T23:45:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27384327725", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Władysław of Legnica, was a Duke of Legnica during 1296–1312, of Brzeg and Wrocław during 1296–1311.", "newValue": "Władysław of Legnica, a scion of the Silesian Piast dynasty, held the ducal territories of Legnica from 1296 to 1312 and simultaneously governed Brzeg and Wrocław from 1296 to 1311. Born into a fragmented political landscape following the death of his father, Henry V the Fat, his reign was characterized by internal strife among his siblings and a broader struggle for autonomy within the Holy Roman Empire's sphere of influence. His ultimate decision to abandon secular rule for a life within the Teutonic Order marks a unique and telling deviation from the typical trajectory of medieval European nobility, reflecting both personal piety and political frustration.

Ascending to power as a minor, Władysław initially ruled under the regency of his mother, Elisabeth of Kalisz, and later his uncle, Bolko I the Strict. Upon reaching majority around 1302, he quickly became embroiled in bitter disputes with his younger brothers, Bolesław III the Generous and Henry VI the Good, concerning the division of their paternal inheritance. These conflicts led to the loss of Wrocław in 1311 to Henry VI and Legnica in 1312 to Bolesław III, effectively ending his direct rule over these key Silesian duchies. His political maneuvering was often overshadowed by the more assertive actions of his siblings, particularly Bolesław III, who sought to consolidate power.

Following his dispossession from secular rule, Władysław made the extraordinary choice to join the Teutonic Order in 1312, an act that transformed his identity from a territorial prince to a monastic knight. This decision, likely driven by a combination of personal conviction and the practical reality of having lost his ducal lands, left no direct dynastic legacy, as he died childless in 1352. His life trajectory illustrates the complex interplay of feudal inheritance, fraternal rivalry, and the powerful spiritual pull of religious orders in high medieval Central Europe."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-11T23:45:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27384327725", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1296-06-06, Legnica"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1296-06-06, Legnica"}], "newValue": ["The death of his father, Henry V the Fat, in 1296 led to Władysław's minority and an initial regency, setting the stage for future power struggles.", "The traditional Piast division of inheritance among male heirs created inherent instability and frequent fraternal disputes over ducal lands like Legnica, Brzeg, and Wrocław.", "The growing influence of the Teutonic Order in Central Europe provided an alternative path for noblemen, including dispossessed rulers, seeking new purpose or power."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-11T23:45:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27384327725", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1352"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1352"}], "newValue": ["Władysław's loss of Wrocław in 1311 further fragmented the Silesian Piast territories, strengthening his brother Henry VI the Good's control over the city.", "His renunciation of secular power and entry into the Teutonic Order in 1312 ended his ducal line and prevented the continuation of his branch of the Piast dynasty.", "The consolidation of Legnica under Bolesław III the Generous after 1312 shifted the balance of power within the Silesian duchies, impacting regional alliances."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-11T23:45:14Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27384327725", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of Legnica", "verb": "RULES", "targetSlug": "field-221", "targetName": "Monarchs & Rulers", "context": "Władysław of Legnica: Sovereign authority in monarchs & rulers"}, {"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of Legnica", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "medieval", "targetName": "Medieval Era", "context": "Władysław of Legnica was active during the Medieval era"}], "newValue": [{"sourceSlug": "henry-v-the-fat", "sourceName": "Henry V the Fat", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "targetName": "Władysław of Legnica", "context": "The death of Henry V the Fat in 1296 directly influenced Władysław's early life as a minor duke under regency."}, {"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of Legnica", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "teutonic-order", "targetName": "Teutonic Order", "context": "Władysław of Legnica participated in the Teutonic Order by joining it as a knight in 1312 after losing his ducal lands."}, {"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of Legnica", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "silesia", "targetName": "Silesia", "context": "The ducal rule and life events of Władysław of Legnica primarily occurred in the historical region of Silesia during the late 13th and early 14th centuries."}, {"sourceSlug": "władysław-of-legnica", "sourceName": "Władysław of L